Let $\mathcal{X}$ be a sample space (typically infinite, e.g., $\mathbb{R}^n$ or a Polish space) equipped with a $\sigma$-finite reference measure $\mu$, and let $\mathcal{M}_{\infty}$ be an infinite-dimensional manifold of probability densities on $(\mathcal{X},\mu)$ (for instance, an Orlicz/Pistone–Sempi exponential statistical manifold, or a suitable Hilbert/Banach manifold of densities). A finite-dimensional statistical model $\mathcal{S}=\{p_\theta:\theta\in\Theta\}$ with $\Theta\subset\mathbb{R}^d$ open is viewed as an immersed (or embedded) submanifold $\iota:\Theta\hookrightarrow \mathcal{M}_\infty$.

In the classical asymptotic theory of parameter estimation (consistency, asymptotic normality, and efficiency of the maximum likelihood estimator and related estimators), one imposes a list of analytic "regularity conditions" on the parametrized family $\theta\mapsto p_\theta$. Typical conditions include:
(i) identifiability of $\theta$;
(ii) common support of $p_\theta$ independent of $\theta$;
(iii) sufficient smoothness of $\theta\mapsto p_\theta(x)$ (e.g., $L^2(\mu)$- or differentiability in quadratic mean);
(iv) existence, finiteness and non-degeneracy (positive-definiteness) of the Fisher information matrix $I(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_\theta[\nabla_\theta\log p_\theta\,\nabla_\theta\log p_\theta^\top]$;
(v) uniform integrability / dominated-convergence type bounds on log-likelihood derivatives;
(vi) interchangeability of differentiation and integration.

Problem. Reformulate each of the regularity conditions (i)–(vi) (and the standard package of analytic hypotheses sufficient for asymptotic normality and efficiency of maximum likelihood estimation) as intrinsic geometric conditions on the submanifold $\iota(\Theta)\subset\mathcal{M}_\infty$. Concretely, find geometric properties of $\iota(\Theta)$ — phrased in terms of the immersion $\iota$, the induced Fisher–Rao metric, the dual $\alpha$-connections of $\mathcal{M}_\infty$, the second fundamental form, completeness, embeddedness, properness, transversality, or the closure/boundary behavior of $\iota(\Theta)$ in $\mathcal{M}_\infty$ — that are equivalent to (or imply, together with minimal additional analytic hypotheses) the standard regularity assumptions used in asymptotic estimation theory. In particular, characterize geometrically the failure modes corresponding to non-regular models such as non-regular exponential families and models with parameter-dependent support.

